{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-economic-moats-and-competitive-advantage",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 1: What an Economic Moat Really Is",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "ROCE Above Cost of Capital: The Financial Fingerprint of a Moat",
          "slug": "roce-above-cost-of-capital-the-financial-fingerprint-of-a-moat",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k001",
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "Profit alone tells you nothing. Profit relative to the capital it took to earn it, compared with what that capital costs, tells you whether a business creates wealth or quietly destroys it."
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k003",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k002",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A company reporting ₹500 crore of profit sounds impressive. If it needed ₹10,000 crore of shareholder and lender money to earn it, that is a 5% return, less than a bank fixed deposit, with far more risk. This lesson gives you the two numbers that expose this: ROCE and the cost of capital. The gap between them is the financial fingerprint of a moat.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k005",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k004",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Step 1: Return on Capital Employed",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k006",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{ROCE} = \\frac{\\text{EBIT}}{\\text{Capital Employed}} \\times 100",
              "caption": "Capital Employed = Shareholders' equity + Borrowings (commonly averaged over opening and closing balances)"
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k013",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k007",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "Earnings Before Interest and Tax. Operating profit before paying lenders and the government. Using EBIT keeps ROCE independent of how the company is financed."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k010",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The total long-term money invested in the business by shareholders and lenders. Often calculated as equity plus borrowings, or total assets minus current liabilities."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k008",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "EBIT",
                  "marks": [
                    "m1l3k007"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k009",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " is used because it is the profit generated by all the capital, before it is split between lenders (interest) and the government (tax). ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k011",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Capital employed",
                  "marks": [
                    "m1l3k010"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k012",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " is all the money shareholders and lenders have put in. The ratio answers one question: for every ₹100 invested in this business, how much operating profit does it generate?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k015",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h3",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k014",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Worked Example: A Hypothetical Paint Company",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k016",
              "_type": "statGrid",
              "stats": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k017",
                  "label": "EBIT",
                  "value": "₹600 crore",
                  "context": "FY operating profit"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k018",
                  "label": "Opening capital employed",
                  "value": "₹2,500 crore",
                  "context": "Equity ₹2,200 cr + debt ₹300 cr"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k019",
                  "label": "Closing capital employed",
                  "value": "₹2,700 crore",
                  "context": "Equity ₹2,450 cr + debt ₹250 cr"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k020",
                  "label": "ROCE",
                  "value": "23.1%",
                  "context": "₹600 cr ÷ average ₹2,600 cr"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k022",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k021",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Average capital employed is (₹2,500 crore + ₹2,700 crore) ÷ 2 = ₹2,600 crore. ROCE is ₹600 crore ÷ ₹2,600 crore = 23.1%. On its own this looks healthy. But healthy compared to what? That is step 2.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k023",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Calculate ROCE",
              "prompt": "A company has EBIT of ₹450 crore, shareholders' equity of ₹1,800 crore, and borrowings of ₹200 crore. Using these closing figures, what is its ROCE?",
              "correctValue": 22.5,
              "tolerance": 0.1,
              "unit": "%",
              "explanation": "Capital employed = ₹1,800 crore + ₹200 crore = ₹2,000 crore. ROCE = ₹450 crore ÷ ₹2,000 crore × 100 = 22.5%."
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k025",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k024",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Step 2: The Cost of Capital",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k030",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k027",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The average return required by all providers of capital, weighted by how much each contributes. Debt cost is taken after tax because interest is tax-deductible."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k026",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Shareholders and lenders both expect a return. Lenders get interest. Shareholders expect a return high enough to compensate for the risk of owning equity. The blended minimum return a business must earn to satisfy both is its ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k028",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "weighted average cost of capital (WACC)",
                  "marks": [
                    "m1l3k027"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k029",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k031",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{WACC} = \\frac{E}{E+D} \\times K_e + \\frac{D}{E+D} \\times K_d \\times (1 - t)",
              "caption": "E = equity, D = debt, Ke = cost of equity, Kd = pre-tax cost of debt, t = tax rate"
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k032",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "K_e = R_f + \\beta \\times \\text{ERP}",
              "caption": "CAPM: risk-free rate plus beta times the equity risk premium"
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k033",
              "_type": "collapsible",
              "title": "Show me the math: WACC for a typical Indian listed company",
              "content": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k035",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k034",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Risk-free rate (Rf): assume a 10-year Government of India bond yield of 6.5%. Check the current yield on the RBI or CCIL website before using it.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k037",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k036",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Beta: assume 1.0, meaning the stock moves broadly in line with the Nifty 50.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k039",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k038",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Equity risk premium (ERP): assume 6%, within the range commonly used for India.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k041",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k040",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Cost of equity: 6.5% + 1.0 × 6% = 12.5%.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k043",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k042",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Pre-tax cost of debt: 9%. Tax rate: 25.17% (22% base rate under Section 115BAA, plus 10% surcharge and 4% cess). After-tax cost of debt: 9% × (1 − 0.2517) = 6.73%.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k045",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k044",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Capital mix: 80% equity, 20% debt.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k047",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "m1l3k046",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "WACC = 0.80 × 12.5% + 0.20 × 6.73% = 10.00% + 1.35% = 11.35%, or roughly 11.3%.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ],
                  "listItem": "bullet",
                  "level": 1
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k048",
              "_type": "donutChart",
              "title": "Illustrative WACC build-up: contribution by source (percentage points)",
              "data": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k049",
                  "label": "Equity: 80% × 12.5%",
                  "value": 10
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k050",
                  "label": "Debt: 20% × 6.73% after tax",
                  "value": 1.35
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k051",
              "_type": "keyFact",
              "label": "Working benchmark",
              "value": "WACC of roughly 11% to 13% for most Indian listed companies",
              "context": "Higher for small caps, volatile businesses, and companies with high beta. Lower for large, stable, low-beta firms."
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k053",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k052",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Step 3: Compare Like with Like",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k055",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k054",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Here is a trap most investors fall into. The ROCE on Screener.in is based on EBIT, which is before tax. WACC is an after-tax number. Comparing them directly flatters every company. Convert ROCE to a post-tax figure first.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k056",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Post-tax ROCE} = \\text{ROCE} \\times (1 - t)",
              "caption": "At a 25.17% tax rate, multiply pre-tax ROCE by roughly 0.75"
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k058",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k057",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A pre-tax ROCE of 15% becomes 15% × 0.7483 = 11.2% post-tax, which is right around a typical Indian WACC. That is why 15% pre-tax ROCE is a useful quick hurdle when scanning Screener.in: below it, a business is at best breaking even on its cost of capital. The paint company's 23.1% becomes about 17.3% post-tax, a spread of roughly 6 percentage points over an 11.3% WACC.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k059",
              "_type": "chart",
              "chartType": "bar",
              "title": "Illustrative: Post-Tax ROCE vs WACC for Four Hypothetical Companies",
              "xAxisLabel": "Company",
              "yAxisLabel": "Percent",
              "data": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k060",
                  "label": "Consumer brand"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k061",
                  "label": "IT services"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k062",
                  "label": "Steel maker"
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k063",
                  "label": "Airline"
                }
              ],
              "series": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k064",
                  "label": "Post-tax ROCE",
                  "values": [
                    24,
                    20,
                    9,
                    5
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k065",
                  "label": "WACC",
                  "values": [
                    11,
                    11.5,
                    12.5,
                    13
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k067",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k066",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Step 4: Turn the Spread into Rupees",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k072",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k069",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "Profit left after charging for the full cost of all capital used, including the return shareholders expect. Also called Economic Value Added (EVA)."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k068",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The spread between post-tax ROCE and WACC, multiplied by capital employed, gives ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k070",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "economic profit",
                  "marks": [
                    "m1l3k069"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k071",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ": the value created after paying every provider of capital their required return.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k073",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Economic Profit} = (\\text{Post-tax ROCE} - \\text{WACC}) \\times \\text{Capital Employed}"
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k074",
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Bigger profit, less value: two hypothetical companies",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Company A",
                "Company B"
              ],
              "rows": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k075",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Capital employed",
                    "₹1,000 crore",
                    "₹5,000 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k076",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Post-tax ROCE",
                    "22%",
                    "11%"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k077",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Post-tax operating profit",
                    "₹220 crore",
                    "₹550 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k078",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "WACC",
                    "12%",
                    "12%"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k079",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Capital charge (WACC × capital)",
                    "₹120 crore",
                    "₹600 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k080",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Economic profit",
                    "+₹100 crore",
                    "−₹50 crore"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k082",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k081",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Company B reports two and a half times the profit of Company A and destroys value every year. Company A creates ₹100 crore of value on a fifth of the capital. A moat is what lets a company stay in column A for decades.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k083",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Calculate Economic Profit",
              "prompt": "A company has capital employed of ₹3,000 crore, a pre-tax ROCE of 20%, a tax rate of 25%, and a WACC of 12%. What is its economic profit in ₹ crore?",
              "correctValue": 90,
              "tolerance": 1,
              "unit": "₹ crore",
              "explanation": "Post-tax ROCE = 20% × (1 − 0.25) = 15%. Spread = 15% − 12% = 3%. Economic profit = 3% × ₹3,000 crore = ₹90 crore."
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k085",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k084",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Why Not Just Use ROE?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k087",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k086",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Return on equity is useful, but it can be inflated by borrowing. Two companies running identical businesses can show very different ROE purely because one uses more debt.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k088",
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Same business, same ROCE, different ROE (tax rate 25.17%, interest at 9%)",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "Firm X (no debt)",
                "Firm Y (60% debt)"
              ],
              "rows": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k089",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Capital employed",
                    "₹1,000 crore",
                    "₹1,000 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k090",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Equity / Debt",
                    "₹1,000 cr / ₹0",
                    "₹400 cr / ₹600 cr"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k091",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "EBIT (15% ROCE)",
                    "₹150 crore",
                    "₹150 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k092",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Interest",
                    "₹0",
                    "₹54 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k093",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Profit before tax",
                    "₹150 crore",
                    "₹96 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k094",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Profit after tax",
                    "₹112.2 crore",
                    "₹71.8 crore"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k095",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "ROE",
                    "11.2%",
                    "18.0%"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k096",
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "Firm Y's 18% ROE is not a sign of a better business. It is the same business carrying more risk. ROCE strips out that leverage effect, which is why it is the better first test for a moat. The exception is banks and NBFCs, where borrowing is the raw material of the business. For them, use ROE and ROA, covered later in the course."
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k098",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k097",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Where ROCE Can Mislead You",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k101",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k099",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Large cash balances: ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k100",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "idle cash sits in capital employed but earns little, dragging ROCE down. Cash-rich companies can have a stronger core business than headline ROCE suggests.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k104",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k102",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Heavy expansion: ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k103",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "capital work-in-progress on a new plant adds to capital employed before it adds to EBIT, temporarily depressing ROCE.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k107",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k105",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "One-off gains: ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k106",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "large other income or asset sale gains can inflate EBIT for a year. Check whether ROCE is driven by operations.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "listItem": "bullet",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "m1l3k110",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k108",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Acquisitions: ",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "m1l3k109",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "goodwill from buying another company inflates capital employed, lowering ROCE even if the acquired business is sound.",
